Section 11: Governance and Compliance.

This section will cover objectives 5.1 and 5.4 of the Sec+ exam. Modules within this section are:

Governance: Covers the general idea of governance, how it impacts an organization and why it is important. Generally useful information but not something I need to dive too deep into. 

Governance Structures: Talked about boards, subcommitee's, government entities, etc. Not much value here.

Policies: Covers documents like AUP, Information Security Policies (such as data classification, access control, encryption, physical security), Business Continuity / Disaster Recovery, Incident Response policy, Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC), etc.

Standards: Talks about different types of organizational standards such as Password standards, access control standards, physical security and also encryption standards. Standards are an integral part of an organization's governance framework.

Procedures: A systemic sequence of actions or steps taken to achieve a certain outcome. Wordy definition from Dion but makes sense. Things like Fire Safety or Evacuation procedure, or data backup procedures.

Governance Considerations: Considerations entail regulatory, legal, industry, and geographical boundaries.

Compliance: Compliance is detailed as agreement and following stipulations provided by regulatory bodies and internal requirements documents alike. Compliance includes reporting and monitoring, performing due diligence and due care activities, as well as attestations and acknowledgements.

Non-compliance Consequences: non-compliance consequences include financial, reputational, contractual impacts, loss of license, etc. 

